  "Question": "A shop advertises that everything is “half price in today’s sale.” In addition, a coupon gives a \\(20\\%\\) discount on sale prices. Using the coupon, the price today represents what percentage discount off the original price?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( 10 \\)",
    "B": "\\( 33 \\)",
    "C": "\\( 40 \\)",
    "D": "\\( 60 \\)",
    "E": "\\( 70 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Scaling the original price scales both savings by the same factor, so it does not change the percentage discount. We may therefore use an original price of \\(\\$100\\).\n\nHalf price makes the sale price \\(\\$50\\). The coupon then saves \\(20\\%\\) of this sale price:\n\\[\n0.20\\cdot50=10.\n\\]\nThe final price is \\(50-10=40\\) dollars. The total savings are \\(100-40=60\\) dollars out of the original \\(100\\), a \\(\\boxed{60\\%}\\) discount, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
